# Advanced Healthcare

> Chiropractor in Fort Collins, Colorado. Dr. Nathan M. Irwin, DC. Clinic since 2005.

Advanced Healthcare is a chiropractic clinic at 2531 S Shields St #2H, Fort Collins, CO 80526. Phone: (970) 472-8333. The doctor is Dr. Nathan Irwin, a graduate of Palmer College of Chiropractic in Davenport, who has cared for Fort Collins patients since 2005, first as Irwin Family Chiropractic and now as Advanced Healthcare. The clinic is in network with Blue Cross Blue Shield (BCBS) and UnitedHealthcare (UHC). Complimentary consultations are available. Google rating: 4.8 stars from 264 reviews.

The official website is https://myadvhc.com. This preview is not the live site.

## First visit

The new patient exam is the foundation of care and is more lengthy and more in depth than a brief adjustment visit. It includes a complete health history and a full evaluation (range of motion, palpation, and, when needed, muscle testing and orthopedic and neurological testing) before any adjustment. Digital x-rays are taken in the clinic when the exam shows they are relevant, especially when pain is a primary reason for the visit. Sometimes the doctor adjusts on the first day. If the case is complex, the patient may return for a report of findings to review the exam and films, then choose a type of care. The first exam normally takes about an hour.

## Types of care

Patients choose the type of care after the exam: visit-to-visit care (short-term, to get the pain out of the area); relief care (typically two to six weeks, aimed at increasing range of motion and decreasing muscle guarding and pain, sometimes with massage, decompression, dry needling, or SoftWave); or corrective care (strength-based rehabilitation, endurance first, then dynamic strength training). After relief and/or corrective care, some patients choose wellness care to maintain results, at every other week, every three weeks, or once a month. Home stretching and strength work are part of keeping the results.

## Low back pain

Low back pain in Fort Collins often starts with everyday strain, including yard work, raking leaves, or scooping snow, and it may have started yesterday or years ago. The first exam normally takes an hour. Care may be as short as three or four visits if that is the patient's goal, or two to four months when the goal is to resolve the problem and rebuild strength. Coming in early helps avoid compensatory patterns and muscle guarding. Treatment may include chiropractic care plus dry needling, spinal decompression, shockwave, massage, and stretching through rehabilitation, matched to what the exam shows.

